I"ˆ<h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

<p>Medical AI is usually tested on multiple-choice questions, where only the final choice counts. But a doctor also needs to know why. Can models explain medical knowledge well?</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>We built MedExQA, a benchmark across five medical specialties that are underrepresented in existing datasets, with multiple explanations for each question and answer. To widen the choice of open medical models, which are mostly based on Llama 2, we also built MedPhi-2, a model based on the small Phi-2 model with 2.7 billion parameters.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>

<p>Judging generated explanations against several reference explanations agrees better with human assessment than conventional scoring. In speech language pathology, current models including GPT-4 lack good understanding. MedPhi-2 outperformed medical models based on Llama2-70B at generating explanations.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>Explanations are a better test of understanding than final answers alone, and small open models can be competitive where resources are limited.</p>
